{
  "dom_id": "step-q342581",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,4)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(4) & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
